1
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:23,700
 Stars, galaxy, in astral seas abound, on cosmic paths we'll trace.

2
00:00:24,020 --> 00:01:33,000
 Man, we're wanked, is such a wondrous thing.

3
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:40,100
 Behind, doofs of rumsey, come take your place.

4
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:55,150
 We're leaving soon for-

5
00:01:55,150 --> 00:02:10,540
 Explored so far and still nothing to report.

6
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:14,930
 It's like it's the same for all the other spacecraft combing this sector.

7
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:21,040
 You suppose the Cassapians are making less trouble since learning their lesson on the green planet?

8
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:22,750
 Don't bet on it.

9
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:24,960
 The Cassapians are real bad.

10
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:26,810
 I hate thinking about them.

11
00:02:27,610 --> 00:02:31,350
 As far as I'm concerned, you're 100% right.

12
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:34,650
 You know, it's kind of nice having you as a co-pilot.

13
00:02:35,210 --> 00:02:37,530
 What's a nice girl like you doing as a space pilot?

14
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:40,100
 Well, the flying part is easy.

15
00:02:40,390 --> 00:02:44,500
 Just learn a little astronomy and some math, and the computer does all the rest.

16
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:46,280
 A basically inaccurate remark.

17
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:50,370
 Computers have memory banks containing the greater part of man's knowledge, true.

18
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:53,370
 However, in space, the unexpected is what we anticipate,

19
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:56,150
 and no machine replaces the human capacity to react.

20
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:58,100
 Sounds just like the professor.

21
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:00,850
 But you still want to go on studying geology.

22
00:03:01,270 --> 00:03:02,550
 Yes, I want to go on.

23
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,260
 Geology concerns tangible objects, things you can touch.

24
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:11,810
 I guess it sort of balances out all of the other confusing abstractions no one really understands.

25
00:03:12,130 --> 00:03:13,560
 They scare me a little.

26
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:19,480
 Well, in the meantime, co-pilot, how about plotting a course toward planet number 4 on our circuit?

27
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:25,740
 Listed in Galactic Group 7052, identification number NGC18.

28
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:27,810
 Oh, do we have to go there?

29
00:03:28,090 --> 00:03:29,450
 Sure we do. Why shouldn't we?

30
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:34,310
 I don't know, but we shouldn't. It's dangerous. I can't say why, but I've got a hunch.

31
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:39,040
 Sorry, Psyche, but we have orders, and we'd better obey them.

32
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:40,630
 Okay, let's go.

33
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:43,100
 Set course for open constellation 7052.

34
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,100
 Straight ascent, 14 minutes, 30 seconds.

35
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:46,660
 Declination, north, 6 degrees.

36
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,280
 Co-pilot, plot our flight course.

37
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:49,740
 Yes, Commander.

38
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,130
 Star cluster 7052, co-pilot Psyche is right.

39
00:03:54,510 --> 00:03:56,810
 It is better to avoid young stars like these.

40
00:03:56,870 --> 00:03:59,270
 It is uncertain exactly how they have evolved.

41
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:01,280
 There are many unknown variables.

42
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:02,680
 It could be very...

43
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:14,520
 Young stars are forming a stable cluster.

44
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:18,280
 If they move in the same direction and at the same speed, no problem.

45
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,800
 But if one falls into the gravitational field of another, it can unleash immense energy.

46
00:04:23,310 --> 00:04:24,190
 Be very careful.

47
00:04:24,390 --> 00:04:29,540
 When matter is violently drawn back upon itself, a billion times more energy is re-e-e-e-e-e-e-e-e-e!

48
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:35,020
 I can't stir it.

49
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:35,920
 The ship's out of control.

50
00:04:37,370 --> 00:04:38,760
 No response!

51
00:04:39,770 --> 00:04:41,350
 This is Dragonfly.

52
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:42,390
 This is Dragonfly.

53
00:04:42,670 --> 00:04:45,210
 We're caught in a giant gravitational storm.

54
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:46,610
 Spaceship damaged.

55
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:48,310
 Control no longer functioning.

56
00:04:48,750 --> 00:04:52,690
 Being pulled into the gravitational field of a planet on Star Cluster 7052.

57
00:04:53,090 --> 00:05:04,580
 Our position is, regardless of what Metro says,

58
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:07,220
 I hope our computer is programmed to handle things like this.

59
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:08,610
 No, no, no, no problem.

60
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:10,050
 We've had it.

61
00:05:25,670 --> 00:05:50,470
 Person of my importance.

62
00:05:51,090 --> 00:05:51,830
 Well, that's better.

63
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:54,200
 Now then, just what is the situation?

64
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:55,560
 No, my knee joints.

65
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:57,040
 Why couldn't I have been made a newer model?

66
00:05:58,190 --> 00:06:00,890
 My, my, human beings are such fragile creatures.

67
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:03,100
 Well, as usual, I know what to do.

68
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,420
 As you say, hang in there, Peter.

69
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:11,990
 Ah, Psyche, you all right?

70
00:06:11,990 --> 00:06:13,830
 Oh, there she's coming, too.

71
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:16,780
 You scared me, you know that?

72
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:27,870
 Going to function?

73
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:30,060
 No, it's useless. Nothing's working on the ship.

74
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:32,940
 If we could repair this crate, you reckon it'd take how long?

75
00:06:33,190 --> 00:06:36,910
 Initial estimates, three months in a spaceport dock with specialized technicians.

76
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:38,950
 And that is the strict minimum.

77
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:40,550
 The radio's out, too.

78
00:06:41,230 --> 00:06:43,720
 And our computer's a nice pile of junk.

79
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:45,830
 We're lucky you made it.

80
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:49,480
 We're lucky the computer worked okay, or we wouldn't be here now.

81
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:52,820
 Well, from now on, Psyche, when you got a hunch, I'll pay attention.

82
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:54,040
 You were absolutely right.

83
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:56,280
 Yeah, okay. But what do we do now?

84
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:01,900
 Logically, correct procedure is wait here until Omega locates us and sends help.

85
00:07:02,470 --> 00:07:03,810
 He's right. We wait.

86
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:06,830
 And hope that Master Control on Omega heard our signal,

87
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:10,910
 and that they can locate this planet from all the rest in this star sector.

88
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:14,910
 So for the time being, we might as well be as comfortable as possible.

89
00:07:16,510 --> 00:07:19,200
 I wouldn't mind if the air conditioning still worked.

90
00:07:20,570 --> 00:07:23,020
 Like a furnace.

91
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:26,650
 How lucky can you get we land on a planet where the sun never sets?

92
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:29,620
 Crazy, erratic cycle.

93
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:31,650
 The heat's terrific.

94
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:34,190
 I think Medro should check it out and report back.

95
00:07:34,190 --> 00:07:36,810
 Always when drudgery's to be done.

96
00:07:37,090 --> 00:07:37,670
 Guess who?

97
00:07:37,790 --> 00:08:27,370
 We shall now furnish report requested. We are at present located in the northern sector of this miserable planet in full exposure to the rays of its circumpolar sun.

98
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:32,850
 Blazing star, if I may so name it, describes one elliptical orbit every 38 hours.

99
00:08:33,560 --> 00:08:37,620
 In short, it never sets, is soon to begin approach phase of cycle, and there's no means of protection.

100
00:08:38,210 --> 00:08:42,110
 So if I'm reading you right, what you're saying is we'd cook if we went out there.

101
00:08:42,870 --> 00:08:47,820
 And we'll roast if we stay in before the red sun gets any closer. Why don't we leave?

102
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:51,210
 In the Southern Hemisphere, it's cooler. We could try to get there.

103
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:52,490
 Leave the ship?

104
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:55,250
 Yes, but then how would they ever find us, Metro?

105
00:08:55,510 --> 00:08:59,050
 If they come in close enough, they'll be able to follow our path with InfraVision.

106
00:08:59,390 --> 00:09:02,670
 And their radio receivers can pick up voices at 10,000 miles.

107
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,010
 What do you think, Metro? Is this planet inhabited?

108
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:07,300
 Not possess sufficient data to reply.

109
00:09:07,870 --> 00:09:11,850
 According to reports issued by the Andromedans, the governing power, one planet in 200, is inhabited.

110
00:09:12,750 --> 00:09:15,710
 Better begin to climb. Get on a mountaintop.

111
00:09:15,990 --> 00:09:19,910
 If we can get a fix on three stars, we'll be able to determine our own position.

112
00:09:20,010 --> 00:09:24,350
 With our silk metallic spacesuits on, we'll have some protection against the heat.

113
00:09:24,710 --> 00:09:26,170
 You're right. It's the only way.

114
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:32,040
 Well, it's now or never. We'll have 15 hours to find a shelter from the sun.

115
00:09:32,690 --> 00:09:37,270
 Otherwise, we're going to be roasted chickens, trusting old Metro's calculations to be right.

116
00:09:38,130 --> 00:09:41,330
 My calculations are always right. As you know, young man.

117
00:09:42,270 --> 00:09:44,790
 Let's go.

118
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:55,380
 Both very kind, but how am I supposed to get down that?

119
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:58,200
 No problem. Just keep your cool, young man.

120
00:09:59,630 --> 00:10:04,120
 You filleting predicament for a scholar of my caliber, tied up like a sausage.

121
00:10:04,650 --> 00:10:05,790
 Or am I just nuts and bolts?

122
00:10:05,790 --> 00:10:41,600
 Think we'll find them. Yes, they don't leave their ship. Sun is getting closer. Oh

123
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:55,640
 Nobody bothered to ask me how long this would take an oversight. No doubt. Okay. Okay. Forget it

124
00:10:55,640 --> 00:11:01,340
 This is hardly the time. I'm sure the mountain can't be too far away

125
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:12,220
 We'll find someplace to shield us. Come on. Just a little further. Now what's wrong? I keep slipping

126
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:15,560
 I wasn't built for this

127
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:22,810
 Billy ate it. Oh, the truth is Peter. I don't know if I can make it. I can feel my strength going

128
00:11:22,810 --> 00:11:24,250
 I can't go on much longer.

129
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:41,420
 Let's stop it.

130
00:11:41,740 --> 00:11:43,280
 If we stay here, it's sure death.

131
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:45,660
 No, I won't let you.

132
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:52,480
 What's wrong with her?

133
00:11:53,020 --> 00:11:53,460
 Psyche!

134
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:25,680
 We get here.

135
00:12:26,050 --> 00:12:26,920
 How do you suppose?

136
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:28,500
 Yours truly as usual.

137
00:12:28,970 --> 00:12:31,250
 You both seemed in need of physical assistance,

138
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:34,510
 and I calculated that immediate shelter was the best of all solutions.

139
00:12:34,850 --> 00:12:35,350
 You feel better.

140
00:12:35,690 --> 00:12:36,790
 Yes, thanks, Metro.

141
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:38,080
 We'll be okay now.

142
00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:40,370
 Did I sleep very long?

143
00:12:40,690 --> 00:12:42,330
 14 hours, 3 minutes, 10 seconds.

144
00:12:42,450 --> 00:12:43,770
 In two hours, we can set off again.

145
00:12:43,850 --> 00:12:45,030
 Meanwhile, save your strength.

146
00:12:48,190 --> 00:12:51,410
 Your share sure smells good.

147
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:55,350
 When we get higher and farther to the south, it'll be even better.

148
00:12:55,430 --> 00:12:59,070
 It's too bright here. When we get over to the other slope, we'll check our position.

149
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:26,080
 Okay, let's find another shelter before the sun returns. Let's go.

150
00:13:32,060 --> 00:13:38,820
 There's a path that leads right over to the other slope.

151
00:13:39,250 --> 00:13:42,320
 Why? When there's a perfectly good shelter right over there.

152
00:13:42,670 --> 00:13:45,600
 Look, it's great. What's wrong with this?

153
00:13:45,850 --> 00:14:02,210
 I mustn't kill him. I hated doing that.

154
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:04,280
 It was him or me. You had to.

155
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:07,860
 Realize that you just saved my life? What if he returns?

156
00:14:08,290 --> 00:14:12,240
 We'll keep an eye out. Don't forget, we're invading his territory.

157
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:14,960
 It's not our job to kill him.

158
00:14:15,010 --> 00:14:18,390
 That's right, except in self-defense. And that's just what it was, Psyche.

159
00:14:18,650 --> 00:14:20,530
 Let's forget it and try to get some rest, right?

160
00:14:23,010 --> 00:14:36,320
 It has to be dark to make out the constellations.

161
00:14:36,820 --> 00:14:40,860
 How do you read it, Metro? Your programming puts you way ahead of us humans.

162
00:14:41,170 --> 00:14:45,540
 True, my memory banks classify 200 billion individual stars, and that's just in our own galaxy.

163
00:14:46,220 --> 00:14:54,840
 From the position of Almac, I calculate that there can be no error in concluding that we need not be altogether surprised to find ourselves on the planet Claris.

164
00:14:55,290 --> 00:14:57,350
 You're a handy fellow to have around.

165
00:14:57,350 --> 00:14:58,160
 I know.

166
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:02,000
 Clarus, then welcome to an inhabited little planet.

167
00:15:02,300 --> 00:15:04,760
 Quite so. The Tolgs live here, not an awfully likable group.

168
00:15:05,190 --> 00:15:07,750
 Colonized four centuries ago by legions from Andromeda.

169
00:15:08,340 --> 00:15:10,660
 They're allies of ours. Let's go to the south and we'll be rescued.

170
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:11,840
 Reluctant allies.

171
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:14,220
 Maybe, but they are members of the confederation.

172
00:15:14,550 --> 00:15:15,340
 Come on, let's go.

173
00:15:15,640 --> 00:15:16,440
 Get your orders.

174
00:15:17,370 --> 00:15:20,100
 Meanwhile, nobody bothers to ask my considered opinion.

175
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:25,210
 Look, water!

176
00:15:30,070 --> 00:15:34,090
 Out of hope of escaping.

177
00:15:34,430 --> 00:15:35,840
 No, I'm gonna save him.

178
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:40,430
 No, Peter, don't. Don't kill anything.

179
00:15:40,430 --> 00:15:41,690
 It's their law in their jungle.

180
00:15:41,830 --> 00:15:43,070
 We'll ruin the ecosystem.

181
00:15:43,330 --> 00:15:44,390
 Yeah, but this is slaughter.

182
00:15:44,580 --> 00:15:45,720
 I've got an idea.

183
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:58,460
 Big Baby is a plant eater.

184
00:15:58,540 --> 00:15:59,260
 He's mild as a lamb.

185
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:06,540
 I think he's in love with you.

186
00:16:07,380 --> 00:16:08,700
 Guess what I'm thinking.

187
00:16:16,080 --> 00:16:20,770
 Pioneer in the frontiers of science tied to a prehistoric lump of ambulating protoblasm.

188
00:16:20,850 --> 00:16:21,350
 Some joke.

189
00:16:21,650 --> 00:16:23,170
 This situation is utterly ridiculous.

190
00:16:23,310 --> 00:16:25,690
 It's humiliating, and I hope it's all a bad dream.

191
00:16:47,470 --> 00:16:48,540
 Significance has ever happened.

192
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:52,680
 I keep asking myself, what are we doing on this miserable planet in the first place?

193
00:17:16,410 --> 00:17:55,190
 Timbaltons!

194
00:18:02,650 --> 00:18:07,650
 I suppose you explain why you come to our world.

195
00:18:07,830 --> 00:18:09,180
 Why are you treating us like this?

196
00:18:09,420 --> 00:18:10,640
 We don't come as enemies.

197
00:18:11,540 --> 00:18:13,280
 It's not.

198
00:18:13,620 --> 00:18:15,340
 It is as spies as you come.

199
00:18:15,380 --> 00:18:15,840
 Not true.

200
00:18:17,410 --> 00:18:18,480
 You lie.

201
00:18:19,350 --> 00:18:21,710
 Trust the truth or else you shall die.

202
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:23,170
 Don't shout at him.

203
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:24,960
 You should help us.

204
00:18:25,330 --> 00:18:26,430
 Our ship crashed.

205
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:28,180
 I swear that's the truth.

206
00:18:29,520 --> 00:18:30,840
 Very well.

207
00:18:30,980 --> 00:18:31,520
 Follow us.

208
00:18:32,390 --> 00:18:33,970
 Our leader Gabor will decide.

209
00:18:43,070 --> 00:18:57,370
 Not far now.

210
00:18:57,610 --> 00:19:00,730
 Around and admire the moonshell of our once great city.

211
00:19:01,090 --> 00:19:03,210
 Ah, well, just as I predicted, that explains everything.

212
00:19:03,570 --> 00:19:05,330
 This is the true sun we are circling.

213
00:19:05,330 --> 00:19:08,090
 And the red sun was circling around us.

214
00:19:08,210 --> 00:19:11,570
 and a collection of moons followed in their orbits, modified...

215
00:19:11,570 --> 00:19:12,370
 Look, forget it, okay?

216
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:15,520
 ...by a gravitation, of course, as you wish. I'm no blabbermouth.

217
00:19:43,410 --> 00:19:46,430
 Fortunately, you have chosen a bad time to visit us.

218
00:19:46,570 --> 00:19:49,290
 Why have you come? I want the truth.

219
00:19:49,640 --> 00:19:53,900
 We are members of the Omega Space Police, and our patrol ship crashed here, Gabor.

220
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:58,180
 Space police, you say? Your ship here, you're spies.

221
00:19:58,300 --> 00:20:01,220
 No, I want the truth. I warn you, and I have ways of getting it.

222
00:20:02,210 --> 00:20:08,200
 ...to make us see horrible things that aren't really there.

223
00:20:08,420 --> 00:20:09,530
 You're not impressed at all.

224
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:16,510
 To have certain power.

225
00:20:17,380 --> 00:20:17,880
 Interesting.

226
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:20,060
 We told you the truth.

227
00:20:20,420 --> 00:20:21,960
 We didn't come here as spies.

228
00:20:22,080 --> 00:20:22,980
 Our ship crashed here.

229
00:20:23,350 --> 00:20:24,970
 How innocent are your words.

230
00:20:25,090 --> 00:20:26,400
 Indeed, who would not believe you?

231
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:30,050
 Well, too bad for you, but there's no time to check up on your story.

232
00:20:30,170 --> 00:20:32,390
 Tomorrow morning, two spies will be executed.

233
00:20:32,510 --> 00:20:34,690
 It will do wonders for one.

234
00:20:40,320 --> 00:20:43,810
 In such a hurry.

235
00:20:44,300 --> 00:20:45,240
 A fair question.

236
00:20:45,780 --> 00:20:47,200
 Tomorrow will be a big day.

237
00:20:47,420 --> 00:20:51,220
 The spaceships of our powerful friends are coming to deliver arms to us.

238
00:20:51,890 --> 00:20:52,670
 Cassiopeians, right?

239
00:20:54,340 --> 00:20:55,690
 Yes.

240
00:20:55,870 --> 00:20:57,150
 One more reason to kill you.

241
00:20:57,150 --> 00:21:04,500
 May as well know that with these arms, we will attack Europe, the improminent capital, and destroy it, as they have destroyed our city.

242
00:21:04,950 --> 00:21:08,050
 But for centuries now, your world has had peace and prosperity.

243
00:21:08,510 --> 00:21:09,610
 Yes, and slavery.

244
00:21:10,530 --> 00:21:14,810
 Henceforth, all power is only mine. I alone will rule my kingdom. Take them away.

245
00:21:14,970 --> 00:21:16,630
 We shall say goodbye in the morning.

246
00:21:24,930 --> 00:21:27,580
 We shall overcome.

247
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:16,920
 Quickly.

248
00:22:22,430 --> 00:22:24,870
 Just fine. We mustn't judge my appearances.

249
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:35,050
 All right, so I didn't make it this time.

250
00:22:35,180 --> 00:22:37,290
 But you'll see, we'll get out of this predicament.

251
00:22:37,290 --> 00:23:00,570
 What's this, the execution squad?

252
00:23:03,140 --> 00:23:06,940
 Look, see my glorious, courageous soldiers.

253
00:23:06,940 --> 00:23:20,000
 Traditional, I believe, to grant one last request.

254
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,460
 Yeah, a good spacecraft.

255
00:23:22,460 --> 00:23:24,800
 I have one question.

256
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:28,250
 You and your officers, you look totally different from your people.

257
00:23:28,250 --> 00:23:29,710
 Why is that? How come?

258
00:23:29,710 --> 00:23:31,240
 We are mixture.

259
00:23:31,240 --> 00:23:33,890
 A cross between these people and the Andromedan.

260
00:23:33,890 --> 00:23:35,800
 We are mutants, if you like.

261
00:23:35,800 --> 00:23:39,920
 Better to be superior among our own people than an underling among aliens.

262
00:23:39,920 --> 00:23:41,960
 Here was located the seat of our government.

263
00:23:41,960 --> 00:23:43,940
 Splendid spot for an execution.

264
00:23:43,940 --> 00:23:53,070
 Great.

265
00:23:53,070 --> 00:24:03,450
 It's time for us to take leave of each other.

266
00:24:03,830 --> 00:24:06,640
 You can tell them up there that all the customs were observed.

267
00:24:08,010 --> 00:24:10,910
 And now, to make the day perfect, here come our friends with weapons.

268
00:24:11,390 --> 00:24:27,940
 Go on! Shoot! They will not escape! Shoot them!

269
00:24:29,020 --> 00:24:54,090
 Figures nobody thinks of me.

270
00:24:55,040 --> 00:24:59,080
 Oh, we haven't forgotten you, Metro. You were great. We couldn't have made it without you.

271
00:25:08,270 --> 00:25:19,950
 We started monitoring your conversation when you came outside.

272
00:25:20,070 --> 00:25:23,140
 We had radio contact. We were waiting for the right moment to move in.

273
00:25:23,260 --> 00:25:25,790
 I don't mind telling you I was afraid we'd be too late.

274
00:25:25,790 --> 00:25:28,530
 It's a good thing that Metro's got reflexes like lightning.

275
00:25:32,070 --> 00:25:33,530
 Turning directly to our base, Colonel?

276
00:25:34,230 --> 00:25:37,440
 First, we'll go to Yurok and make a full report.

277
00:25:37,440 --> 00:25:43,360
 Putting their personal ambitions to one side, the fact that the rebels are armed by the Teryg should give us a lead on what to do.

278
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:50,110
 Good thinking, son. And knowing we're here, the Cassipeans wouldn't dare come. Anyway, we're sure the Andromedans are on their guard.

279
00:25:50,110 --> 00:25:52,670
 Perhaps these people are sincere in their struggle.

280
00:25:52,670 --> 00:26:03,770
 Perhaps, but the Andromedans are members of the Confederation, our allies.

281
00:26:03,770 --> 00:26:18,590
 So what?

